  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem using a hybrid optimization approach combining global and local search methods. The core idea is to leverage a multi-resolution search strategy starting from the parent solution, progressively increasing the number of samples while maintaining the feasibility constraints. This approach is motivated by the observation that higher-resolution representations can better capture subtle features of the optimal function, potentially leading to improved overlap bounds. Unlike previous methods that relied primarily on local optimization, we incorporate restarts with diverse initialization techniques and dimensionally-aware parameter tuning to avoid local optima.\n\n   The strategy involves:\n   - Coarse-to-fine resolution optimization (starting from parent's resolution 380 samples)\n   - Global search with random restarts guided by population-based techniques\n   - Local refinement using stabilized SLSQP with analytical gradients\n   - Dynamic hyperparameter scaling based on problem dimensions\n   - Dimensionality-adjusted convergence criteria\n   - Careful handling of integral constraints and boundaries\n\n   Limitations include the risk of numerical instability in nonlinear optimization and the curse of dimensionality at higher resolutions. The effectiveness relies on maintaining a balance between exploration (global search) and exploitation (local refinement) while respecting the integral constraint and box bounds.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   The optimization problem is defined on a compact set D = ([0,2] \u2229 h)\u00b2 with h(x) \u2208 [0,1] and \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1.\n\n   Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_{k\u2208\u211d} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k))dx\n\n   Discretization:\n   - For n-point samples: x_i = i\u00b7(2/n) for i=0..n-1\n   - Objective approximation: max_lag(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))\u00b7(2/n)\n   - Constraint: \u2211h_i\u00b7(2/n)=1 \u2192 \u2211h_i = n/2 exactly\n   - Individual constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\n   Key representation elements:\n   - Discretized function h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with \u2211h_i = n/2\n   - Lag configurations: 2n-1 different alignments\n   - Integral normalization: preserves constant volume\n\n   The optimization formulation uses:\n      Minimize: max\u2096 (h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k))) difference over lags\n      Subject to:\n        0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2200i\n        \u2211h_i = n/2\n\n   3. Implementation Procedure\nImplementation requires:\n1. Initial Setup\n   - Start time tracking\n   - Random seed initialization\n   - Parent solution projection (if available) to feasible set\n   - Budget management (1100 seconds)\n\n2. Resolution Handling\n   - n_list = [380, 760, 1520, 3040] preserving relative spacing\n   - For each n in n_list:\n      * Generate candidate if feasible time remains\n      * Interpolate maintain feasible integral\n      * Global search: PSO with diversity-maintaining mutation\n      * Local refinement: SLSQP with explicit integral constraint handling\n      * Score computation using mode='full' correlation\n\n3. Optimization Subroutine\n   def optimize_resolution(n):\n      x0 = interpolated_parent(n)\n      bounds = [(0,1)]*n\n      integral_constraint = {'type':'eq','fun':lambda x: np.sum(x)-n/2}\n      - Global phase (PBT):\n          pop = initialize_population(x0, diversity=True)\n          for gen in range(max_global_gen):\n             fitness = [objective(x,n) for x in pop]\n             best_idx = np.argmin(fitness)\n             new_pop = perturb_best(pop, best_idx, diversity_level)\n             if improvement threshold met: break\n      - Local phase (SLSQP):\n          cons = {'type':'eq','fun':integral_constraint}\n          res = minimize(objective, local_x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=cons, ...)\n          x_final = post_processing(res.x,n)\n          c5 = objective(x_final,n)\n\n4. Score Evaluation\n   def objective(x,n):\n      h = np.array(x)\n      corr = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')\n      return np.max(corr)*(2.0/n)\n\n   Strict feasibility checks ensure:\n   - 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n   - np.sum(h) \u2248 n/2 within tolerance\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Primary resolution sequence: [380, 760, 1520, 3040]\n   - Optimization hybrids:\n      * PSO-local SLSQP\n      * Differential evolution-base\n      * Random restarts (10-20 per resolution)\n   - Dimension-based parameters:\n        n=380: PSO pop=40, SLSQP nit=300\n        n=760: PSO pop=30, SLSQP nit=200\n        n=1520: PSO pop=20, SLSQP nit=100\n        n=3040: PSO pop=10, SLSQP nit=50\n   - Objective averaging: Compute internal consistency\n        avg_corr = (np.sum(corr[:-1]) + corr[-1]*0.5) / len(corr)\n\n5. Numerical Robustness\n   - Integral maintenance: Iterative clipping with adjustment\n   def ensure_integral(x,n):\n        current = np.sum(x)\n        target = n/2\n        while abs(current-target) > 1e-6*n:\n            excess = np.argsort(x)[::-1][:int(abs(current-target)*0.5)]\n            x[excess] -= np.sign(target-current)*0.01\n            current = np.sum(x)\n        return np.clip(x,0,1)\n\n   - Gradient estimation: Central finite difference for SLSQP\n   - Convergence monitoring: Track inter-generational diversity\n\n6. Runtime Safeguards\n   - Total time budget: 1100 seconds\n   - Wall-clock monitoring: abort if (time.time()-start_time) > budget_s\n   - Feasibility verification: Post-processing and projection\n   - Progressive escalation: Start lower resolutions if higher time wasted\n   - Return contract:\n        h_values: Last feasible solution\n        c5_bound: Best achieved value\n        n_points: Effective resolution used\n\n7. Helper Functions\n   - Interpolation:\n        def resample_to(n):\n            x_base = np.linspace(0,2,380)\n            x_new = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n            return np.interp(x_new,x_base,initial_h_values)\n   - Objective wrapper:\n        def obj_for_minimize(x,n):\n            return objective(x,n)\n   - Projection:\n        def project_feasible(x,n):\n            x_clip = np.clip(x,0,1)\n            x_feas = np.copy(x_clip)\n            cum_sum = np.cumsum(x_feas-0.5)\n            diff = n/2 - np.sum(x_clip)\n            while diff > 1e-6:\n                idx = np.argmax(cum_sum)\n                x_feas[idx] += np.sign(diff)*np.min(1-x_feas[idx], 0.5*abs(diff))\n                cum_sum[idx] += x_feas[idx]-0.5\n                diff -= np.sign(diff)\n            return x_feas",
